# ── Telegram Bot (apply at @BotFather) — OPTIONAL ─────────────────
# Telegram is only the mobile notification / deep-link rail. Both lines below
# are commented out by default: the service starts without them and serves the
# Web Console (see the Web Console section further down). Uncomment and fill
# BOTH to enable the bot. A configured TELOXIDE_TOKEN also switches off the
# printed local login link unless AEGIS_LOCAL_LOGIN_TOKEN is set.
# TELOXIDE_TOKEN=123456789:ABCdefGHIjklmNOPqrstUVWxyz
# ADMIN_CHAT_ID=123456789
#
# Local-only workbench (no Telegram): set ADMIN_CHAT_ID to a non-zero integer
# so /webapp/vnext/* has a chat context. TELOXIDE_TOKEN can stay unset.
# first-run.sh and deploy.sh write ADMIN_CHAT_ID=1 when the token is unset.
# They do not write TELOXIDE_TOKEN and do not start the bot.
# ADMIN_CHAT_ID=1

# ── Reasoner (required: grok / Codex / API key) ──────────────
# Ask the human in this order: grok login, then Codex login, then
# a usable OPENAI_API_KEY (the placeholder below is rejected).
#   1) grok build CLI + `grok login --device-auth` (~/.grok/auth.json)
#      curl -fsSL https://x.ai/cli/install.sh | bash
#      Satisfies startup, grok -p and run_external; realtime voice is deferred.
#      Owner chat still needs Settings → Grok → Connect → Use for chat
#      (in-app xAI device code; not the same file as grok CLI auth.json).
#   2) Codex CLI + Settings → Codex → Connect → Use for chat
#   3) a real OPENAI_API_KEY — last resort (OpenRouter / Ollama / …)
# Unset llm_provider keeps the legacy openai_compat chat path.
# Subscription binding enables the matching executor unless explicitly disabled.
# ~/.grok/auth.json does not survive a new Cloud VM.
#
# ── LLM via OpenRouter (recommended when using an API key) ────
# SECRET — stays in env (never migrated to runtime settings).
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-xxxxxxxx
# URL — stays in env (infrastructure wiring, not user-reconfigurable).
OPENAI_API_BASE=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
#
# LLM_MODEL below is a FIRST-BOOT BOOTSTRAP default only, and only for the
# OpenRouter / openai_compat API-key path. On every start the load path is:
#     runtime setting `llm_model` (SQLite) > LLM_MODEL env > empty
# Empty means the selected provider's own default (`xai_subscription_model` /
# `codex_model` / OpenRouter catalog pick). Grok-first / Codex-first installs
# should leave this unset (or empty) so logs do not show a stale API model
# while the router serves grok-4.x. `setup-auth --provider grok|codex` also
# clears the stored `llm_model`. Persistent overrides go through
# `switch_model` / `update_setting` → HITL approval → hot-reload.
# LLM_MODEL=
# For a new OpenRouter API-key setup without a chosen model, first-run.sh
# recommends deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash and verifies it before accepting setup.
# Existing model choices and other API endpoints are preserved. This does not
# enable paid fallback or Jev; both still require separate opt-in.
#
# Optional runtime setting (not env): `model_capability_overrides`.
# JSON shape:
# {"vendor/model":{"input_modalities":["text","image","audio","file","video"],"output_modalities":["text"]}}

# ── Secure Computer Use (Linux shared path; off by default) ─────────
# Master switch. When unset/false, CU tools are not offered and the pipeline refuses work.
# AEGIS_SECURE_CU_ENABLED=0
# Adapter: auto (Linux → linux_atspi) | loopback | linux
# AEGIS_SECURE_CU_ADAPTER=auto
# Device id bound into CU intents (default linux-local / loopback-local).
# AEGIS_SECURE_CU_DEVICE_ID=linux-oracle
# TypeSafe Jev via OpenRouter Decisions API (NOT chat completions):
# Optional separate API billing; Grok/ChatGPT subscriptions do not cover Jev.
# Default OFF, even when API keys are present. Set to 1 to opt in, then restart.
# A saved choice in Settings > Advanced > AI and spending > Jev overrides this.
# AEGIS_JEV_ENABLED=0
# AEGIS_JEV_API_KEY=
# Falls back to OPENROUTER_API_KEY, then legacy OPENAI_API_KEY only if sk-or-*.
# Disabled: public search uses local checks without a repeated setting notice;
# an enabled judge becoming unavailable produces a degradation notice.
# other outbound actions hold, and computer actions use local Ask rules.
#   POST https://openrouter.ai/api/alpha/decisions
# Uses the separate key above, or a configured OpenRouter key as documented.
# Model pin (default typesafe/jev-1.13) or alias ~typesafe/jev-latest:
# AEGIS_JEV_MODEL=typesafe/jev-1.13
# Read-only CU tools (same Intent→Jev→sign→Device path):
#   system.session_probe, system.frontmost_app, system.accessible_tree,
#   system.focused_control, system.screenshot_digest
# Jev allow|ask|deny on non-CU high-risk tools (google_drive_upload). Off = no change.
# AEGIS_JEV_NON_CU_GATE_ENABLED=0
# Optional Oracle hand-test override (only when the gate flag is on):
# AEGIS_JEV_NON_CU_FORCE_VERDICT=ask
# Oracle runbook: docs/secure-computer-use-linux-oracle.md
# Docs: docs/design/secure-computer-use-p0.md

# ── Or local Ollama (air-gapped sovereign deployment) ─────────────
# OPENAI_API_BASE=http://localhost:11434/v1
# LLM_MODEL=llama3.2

# ── Embedding (semantic memory) ─────────────────────────────────
# Grok / Codex installs have no usable OPENAI_API_KEY. Install the local
# Ollama embedding dependency (same approach as the Oracle production host):
#   scripts/ensure-local-embedding.sh
# which writes EMBEDDING_API_BASE/KEY/MODEL/DIMENSIONS for nomic-embed-text
# (768d; matches the L2 lesson threshold calibration). first-run.sh does this
# automatically. Without it the core fails loudly with those install steps —
# it does not silently swallow OpenRouter 401s.
#
# When using an OpenRouter / OpenAI API key, embeddings can reuse that key
# (leave EMBEDDING_* unset). FIRST-BOOT BOOTSTRAP DEFAULTS ONLY otherwise:
# After first start, ALL six embedding keys below are reconfigurable at
# runtime via `update_setting` (HITL-gated user approval). The startup read
# priority is:
#     runtime setting (SQLite)  >  env var here  >  hard-coded default
# On approval, the live `EmbeddingClient` hot-reloads via
# `apply_settings_update`:
#   • embedding_primary_model / embedding_backup_model — atomic mutex swap,
#     surfaces consent alert if corpus is now cross-model
#   • embedding_dimensions — atomic update, REQUIRES corpus reembed
#     (stored vectors of any other dim are incomparable)
#   • embedding_probe_cooldown_secs / embedding_promote_after_secs —
#     atomic update, takes effect on next embed()
# See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md §3.3.1 for the full settings-driven flow.
#
# In production, operators should NOT edit these env entries — they ask
# the LLM to propose the change, approve via Telegram, and the client
# hot-reloads in-process. Env entries here are the bootstrap fallback.
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=openai/text-embedding-3-small
# EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=512
#
# Backup embedding model for primary/backup auto-failover. Must share
# api_base / api_key / dimensions with primary — the two models' vectors
# are otherwise not comparable, and mixing them would poison HNSW
# similarity scores. Write-path callers skip persistence while degraded
# and the background reembed migration fills in NULL-embedding rows on
# restart once primary recovers.
# EMBEDDING_BACKUP_MODEL=baai/bge-small-en
# EMBEDDING_PROBE_COOLDOWN_SECS=60
#
# If primary stays degraded for this many continuous seconds, backup is
# PERMANENTLY promoted to primary and the old primary is abandoned (backup
# slot becomes None). State persists via DB settings
# (embedding_primary_promoted_{from,to,at}) across restarts. Reverting
# requires the `unset_embedding_primary_promotion` tool (user-approved).
# Default 86400 (24h); allowed range [3600, 604800]. Motivation: outages
# >24h usually indicate model deprecation, not transient provider
# failure — auto-escalate to new primary rather than stay in the
# "alerting but serving" state indefinitely. See
# docs/DEPLOYMENT.md §3.3.2 for full behavior + runbook.
# EMBEDDING_PROMOTE_AFTER_SECS=86400

# ── Fabrication Guard ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Model used for the factual-grounding guard (default: same as LLM_MODEL).
# A cheap/fast model is recommended since the guard only does JSON classification.
# GUARD_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini

# ── Logging (runtime-configurable) ────────────────────────────────
# Supports RUST_LOG syntax. Default: info. Can be changed at runtime via
# set_log_level pseudo-tool in Telegram (no restart needed).
# RUST_LOG=info
# RUST_LOG=debug
# RUST_LOG=warn,aegis_runtime_core=debug
# RUST_LOG=info,aegis_runtime_core::orchestrator=trace

# ── Aegis Runtime Settings ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Path to store Ed25519 keypair (default: ./aegis_runtime_identity.key)
# IDENTITY_KEY_PATH=./aegis_runtime_identity.key
# Path to SQLite database (default: ./aegis_runtime_memory.db)
# MEMORY_DB_PATH=./aegis_runtime_memory.db
# Memory and opt-in heirloom DBs use SQLCipher. Inject AEGIS_RUNTIME_DB_KEY_HEX (64
# hex chars) through the process environment/secret manager before starting;
# NEVER store it in this .env or alongside database/backups. Foreground runs
# can instead prompt for a passphrase. See README "Database encryption".
# Maximum database size in GB (default: 20). Writes blocked when exceeded.
# MEMORY_MAX_SIZE_GB=20
# HTTP control panel port (default: 3000)
# AXUM_PORT=3000

# ── Admin API Authentication ────────────────────────────────────
# Bearer token for protected Admin API endpoints (e.g. /skills).
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# If unset, protected endpoints return 403.
# ADMIN_API_TOKEN=your-secret-token-here

# ── Google Workspace (Calendar, Drive — unified OAuth2) ──
# NOTE (2026-09): Google Calendar / Drive / Gmail work is delegated to the
# external executor (Codex) through the `run_external` tool; nothing in this
# block is needed for a first run. The in-process OAuth flow below is kept for
# the legacy calendar/drive tools only.
# Unified Google OAuth2 replaces per-service static tokens.
# One-time setup via Admin API:
#   POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/auth/google/setup
#     Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_API_TOKEN
#     {"client_id":"xxx.apps.googleusercontent.com","client_secret":"xxx"}
# This opens a browser for consent → stores encrypted refresh token.
# Scopes granted: calendar.events, drive.readonly, drive.file
#
# How to get client_id / client_secret:
#   1. Go to https://console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services → Credentials
#   2. Create OAuth Client ID (Desktop application type)
#   3. Enable Google Calendar API and Google Drive API
#   4. Copy the Client ID and Client Secret
#
# Credential storage: encrypted in $CREDENTIAL_DB_PATH (default: ./aegis_runtime_credentials.db)
# CREDENTIAL_DB_PATH=./aegis_runtime_credentials.db
#
# Pre-configure Google OAuth credentials so users can authorize
# Calendar/Drive from Telegram with a single click.
# Create at: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
# App type: "Web application", redirect URI: {AEGIS_PUBLIC_URL}/auth/callback/google
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=xxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPx-xxxxx
#
# Public URL of this deployment — OPTIONAL. Needed only when the console must
# be reachable from outside (Telegram Mini App, Google OAuth redirect URIs).
# When unset, the console listener still starts, bound to 127.0.0.1 on
# OAUTH_CALLBACK_PORT, and the console is at http://127.0.0.1:8765/ui/.
# AEGIS_PUBLIC_URL=https://aegis.example.com
# OAUTH_CALLBACK_PORT=8765
#
# Legacy fallback (still works if Google OAuth2 is not configured):
# GOOGLE_CALENDAR_TOKEN=your-oauth-access-token


# ── GitHub Code Assistant (read-only) ──────────────────────────
# Personal access token with repo:read scope.
# Create at: https://github.com/settings/tokens/new
# Auth header auto-injected into host_fetch for api.github.com.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_xxxxxxxxxxxx

# ── Web Console (owner interface) ────────────────────────────
# Served by the binary at http://127.0.0.1:8765/ui/ (127.0.0.1:OAUTH_CALLBACK_PORT,
# default 8765). Port 3000 (AXUM_PORT) is the bearer-token admin API, not the
# console. The same bundle also opens as a Telegram Mini App when
# AEGIS_PUBLIC_URL is set (Telegram requires an HTTPS public URL).
#
# Build the frontend once (Node 22 + npm; not needed at runtime):
#   cd webapp && npm ci && npm run build
#
# The binary looks for the bundle at ./webapp/dist (relative to the current
# directory), then next to the executable, then the source tree
# (CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR). A missing bundle logs a WARN naming the path it tried.
# Explicit override (deploy.sh sets this in production):
# AEGIS_WEBAPP_DIR=/opt/aegis-runtime/current/webapp/dist
#
# Optional standalone Web Console login via Google OIDC.
# This is separate from Google/Calendar OAuth grants above: it identifies who is
# logging into the Web Console and requests only openid/email/profile scopes.
# Optional advanced login. Local login and remote Passkeys need no Google project.
GOOGLE_LOGIN_ENABLED=false
# GOOGLE_LOGIN_CLIENT_ID=xxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_LOGIN_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPx-xxxxx
# GOOGLE_LOGIN_ALLOWED_EMAILS=user@example.com
# GOOGLE_LOGIN_ALLOWED_DOMAIN=example.com
# GOOGLE_LOGIN_REDIRECT_PATH=/auth/callback/google
# SESSION_COOKIE_SECRET=generate-at-least-32-random-characters
# SESSION_COOKIE_NAME=__Host-aegis_session
# SESSION_TTL_SECS=2592000
# SESSION_IDLE_TTL_SECS=2592000
#
# Local login (no Google project, no Telegram bot): when neither
# GOOGLE_LOGIN_CLIENT_ID nor TELOXIDE_TOKEN is configured, the service generates
# a one-time login token at startup and prints the full link to stderr / the
# journal:
#   http://127.0.0.1:8765/auth/login/local?token=<token>
# Opening it creates the owner session and lands on /ui/. Set a fixed secret to
# make the link stable — this also enables local login when Google login or a
# Telegram bot is configured. `off` disables local login entirely.
# AEGIS_LOCAL_LOGIN_TOKEN=<secret>
# AEGIS_LOCAL_LOGIN_TOKEN=off
#
# Safe production writer for Google Login values; does not print secret values
# and validates the candidate env before replacing the file:
#   GOOGLE_LOGIN_CLIENT_ID=xxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com \
#   GOOGLE_LOGIN_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPx-xxxxx \
#   GOOGLE_LOGIN_ALLOWED_EMAILS=user@example.com \
#   AEGIS_ENV_FILE=/opt/aegis-runtime/shared/.env \
#   scripts/configure-webapp-login.sh
#
# Production preflight:
#   AEGIS_ENV_FILE=/opt/aegis-runtime/shared/.env make webapp-production-config-check
#
# When AEGIS_PUBLIC_URL is set, the LLM gains access to `render_webapp`
# pseudo-tool for opening interactive Mini App UIs in Telegram.

# ── Multimodal (Voice, TTS, Image Generation) ────────────────
# Speech-to-Text (OpenAI-compatible Audio API)
# AEGIS_STT_API_KEY=
# AEGIS_STT_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# AEGIS_STT_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini-transcribe
#
# Realtime voice is deferred in default builds (experimental-realtime-voice only).
#   1) Settings → Connect Grok (preferred)
#   2) Grok CLI token file readable by the service user
#   3) Paid XAI_API_KEY only as last resort
# Service HOME is often /home/aegis-runtime (not the operator's $HOME), so point
# at a shared auth.json the service can read (mode 600, owner=service user):
# AEGIS_GROK_AUTH_JSON=/opt/aegis-runtime/shared/grok-auth.json
# AEGIS_GROK_HOME=/home/ubuntu
# AEGIS_VOICE_MODEL=grok-voice-think-fast-2.0
# AEGIS_VOICE_MAX_SESSION_SECONDS=600
# AEGIS_VOICE_TEST_STUB=1
#
# Text-to-Speech (xAI TTS by default)
# AEGIS_TTS_API_KEY=
# AEGIS_TTS_BASE_URL=https://api.x.ai/v1
# AEGIS_TTS_VOICE=eve
#
# Image Generation
# Direct path uses xAI-compatible /images/generations when AEGIS_IMAGEGEN_API_KEY is set.
# Without that key, generate_image falls back to OpenRouter chat image generation via OPENAI_API_KEY.
# AEGIS_IMAGEGEN_API_KEY=
# AEGIS_IMAGEGEN_BASE_URL=https://api.x.ai/v1
# AEGIS_IMAGEGEN_MODEL=grok-imagine-image-2.0

# ── MCP (Model Context Protocol) Servers ──────────────────────
# Path to JSON config listing external MCP servers to connect via stdio.
# Default: ~/.aegis-runtime/mcp_servers.json or ./mcp_servers.json
# MCP_SERVERS_CONFIG=/path/to/mcp_servers.json
#
# MCP is disabled by default (mcp_enabled=false in runtime settings).
# User must explicitly enable via Telegram approval, then approve
# each server individually on first use.
#
# Example ~/.aegis-runtime/mcp_servers.json:
# {
#   "servers": {
#     "github": {
#       "name": "github",
#       "command": "npx",
#       "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"],
#       "env": {"GITHUB_TOKEN": "ghp_..."},
#       "description": "GitHub issues, PRs, repos"
#     }
#   }
# }

# --- Aegis Executor (read-only diagnostics) -----------------------------------
# Shared bearer token for the executor's /api/v1/diagnostics and job endpoints.
# The core injects it for fetches to AEGIS_EXECUTOR_URL only; the executor
# refuses every diagnostics request when it is unset (fail closed).
# The SAME value must be present in /opt/aegis-runtime/shared/executor.env (the
# executor service's EnvironmentFile). Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
AEGIS_EXECUTOR_TOKEN=
AEGIS_EXECUTOR_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9876

# --- External executor (run_external → grok build / Codex) --------------------
# The `run_external` tool hands one self-contained task to a coding agent CLI
# in a fresh scratch directory and returns a receipt. Subscription binding
# enables it unless explicitly disabled; auto follows chat. All keys optional; the
# defaults shown are compiled in (core/src/external_executor/mod.rs).
# AEGIS_EXECUTOR_GROK_BIN=grok                 # grok build CLI (name on PATH or absolute path)
# AEGIS_EXECUTOR_GROK_MODEL=grok-4.7           # model passed to grok build (ACP)
# AEGIS_EXECUTOR_GROK_EFFORT=high              # reasoning effort: low | medium | high
# AEGIS_EXECUTOR_GROK_SANDBOX=                 # value for grok's --sandbox flag; unset = no flag
# AEGIS_EXECUTOR_CODEX_BIN=codex               # Codex CLI (app-server mode)
# AEGIS_EXECUTOR_CODEX_MODEL=gpt-6-astra       # model passed to Codex
# AEGIS_EXECUTOR_CODEX_EFFORT=high             # reasoning effort: low | medium | high
# AEGIS_EXECUTOR_CODEX_HOME=                   # CODEX_HOME override; unset = reuse codex_cli_home
#
# Parent of the per-run scratch directories (<runs_dir>/<run_id>). Default:
# <directory of MEMORY_DB_PATH>/executor-runs when MEMORY_DB_PATH is set;
# otherwise /opt/aegis-runtime/shared/executor-runs if that directory already
# exists (installs made by scripts/deploy.sh); otherwise ./executor-runs.
# AEGIS_EXECUTOR_RUNS_DIR=
#
# The agent child gets a clean environment: only PATH, HOME, LANG, LC_ALL,
# TERM, XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, TMPDIR and variables starting with GROK_, CODEX_ or
# XAI_ are passed through, excluding API_KEY variables. Aegis secrets stay out.

# --- Local speech-to-text (whisper.cpp) ---------------------------------------
# When AEGIS_STT_LOCAL_BIN + AEGIS_STT_LOCAL_MODEL are set, voice notes are
# transcribed locally (else the API backend above). Keep LANGUAGE=auto: a
# pinned language makes whisper hallucinate on other-language audio.
# AEGIS_STT_LOCAL_BIN=/path/to/whisper-cli
# AEGIS_STT_LOCAL_MODEL=/path/to/ggml-small.bin
# AEGIS_STT_LOCAL_DETECT_MODEL=/path/to/ggml-base.bin
# AEGIS_STT_LOCAL_LANGUAGE=auto

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Variables the code reads that this file previously did not mention.
# (Audited 2026-09-14 by diffing `env("X")` / `env::var("X")` call sites
# in core/ and executor/ against this file; defaults re-verified against
# the code on 2026-09-16.) All are optional unless marked REQUIRED — the
# defaults shown are the ones compiled in.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Embeddings (semantic memory) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Default: the SAME endpoint and key as the LLM (OPENAI_API_BASE /
# OPENAI_API_KEY), so with OpenRouter nothing extra is needed. Set these only
# when embeddings come from a different OpenAI-compatible provider. If the
# endpoint cannot serve EMBEDDING_MODEL the embedder goes degraded and
# semantic recall stops (the assistant still answers, but stops remembering
# across sessions) — the health probe alerts on that transition.
# EMBEDDING_API_BASE=https://api.openai.com/v1
# EMBEDDING_API_KEY=sk-xxxxxxxx

# ── Storage and identity ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# (The SQLite database location is MEMORY_DB_PATH in the Aegis Runtime Settings
# block above; every code path — including pricing refresh — uses that file.)
# OWNER_DID=did:aegis:telegram:123456789   # owner identity; default did:aegis:telegram:<ADMIN_CHAT_ID> (chat id 0 when unset)
# L1_CACHE_SIZE=50                         # messages kept hot in the L1 window (default 50)
# USER_TIMEZONE=Asia/Shanghai              # IANA zone for owner-facing timestamps; default = host zone, UTC when the host has none

# ── Web push (browser notifications) ─────────────────────────────────
# Generate a VAPID keypair once (e.g. `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys`).
# Both the AEGIS_-prefixed and bare names are accepted; set one set.
# AEGIS_WEB_PUSH_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# AEGIS_WEB_PUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# AEGIS_WEB_PUSH_VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# ── Aegis Executor (privilege-separated shell service) ───────────────
# Read by the aegis-executor binary (its EnvironmentFile is
# /opt/aegis-runtime/shared/executor.env), not by the core.
# EXECUTOR_BIND=127.0.0.1:9876           # listen address (loopback only by default)
# AEGIS_PUBKEY_HEX=                      # core's Ed25519 public key (`aegis_runtime_core --print-pubkey`); without it /execute rejects everything

# ── Optional integrations ────────────────────────────────────────────
# AEGIS_CHROME_BIN=/usr/bin/chromium     # browser_fetch; unset = first existing of /opt/google/chrome/chrome, /usr/bin/google-chrome[-stable], /usr/bin/chromium[-browser]; none found = page fetching unavailable
# AEGIS_VOICE_WS_URL=wss://api.x.ai/v1/realtime   # realtime voice websocket (default shown)
# AEGIS_XAI_API_BASE=https://api.x.ai/v1           # xAI API base for x_search (default shown)
# XAI_SUBSCRIPTION_API_BASE=https://api.x.ai/v1    # xAI base for the Grok-subscription backend (default shown)
# OPENROUTER_MGMT_KEY=                   # OpenRouter management API (cost reporting only)
# OAUTH_CALLBACK_BIND=127.0.0.1          # interface for the console / OAuth callback listener (default loopback)
# AEGIS_TELEGRAM_POLLING=1               # 0 disables long-polling (webhook/None deployments); anything else = enabled
# AEGIS_WEBAPP_GENERATED_IMAGE_DIR=      # default <directory of MEMORY_DB_PATH>/webapp_generated/images
# AEGIS_WEBAPP_GENERATED_AVATAR_DIR=     # default <directory of MEMORY_DB_PATH>/webapp_generated/avatars
#
# Hercules delegation (`delegate_task`). Default: OFF. Requires your own Hercules
# server (https://github.com/AAlpha7/hercules-public); see README.
# Credentials alone do not enable the tool or the delegation prompt.
# 安琪 uses run_external (auto follows the chat subscription; no fallback by default) unless you
# explicitly opt in. Leave unset or 0 for open-source / default deploy.
# AEGIS_HERCULES_DELEGATION_ENABLED=0
# HERCULES_API_BASE=                     # Hercules API base URL (ignored unless flag is on)
# HERCULES_TOKEN=                        # bearer token for that API
# HERCULES_ENV=                          # staging | production; unset = guessed from the URL (:8788 or "staging" = staging)
#
# Run the binary as an MCP stdio server (tools only, no assistant loop):
# AEGIS_MCP_SERVER=1
#
# Repo the owner-only WebApp debug job runner executes smoke scripts from.
# Default: the source tree at build time (CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR/..), else cwd.
# AEGIS_REPO_ROOT=/path/to/aegis-runtime

# ── Heirloom subsystem (experimental) ────────────────────────────────
# Disabled by default. See docs/HEIRLOOM.md. Experimental wiring uses
# SoftEnclave::new_dev — DEV custody only (fixed dev seed in process memory,
# plaintext *internal* payload codec, permissive ethics validator). The
# heirloom DB file is SQLCipher-encrypted; the development seams do not
# provide hardware key custody or confidentiality against a running host.
# AEGIS_RUNTIME_HEIRLOOM_BRIDGE=on           # ONLY literal `on` enables it; unset/off/other values leave existing files untouched
# HEIRLOOM_EVENT_LOG_DB=                    # used ONLY when enabled; default next to MEMORY_DB_PATH, named heirloom_event_log.db
# AEGIS_RUNTIME_PROFILE_READ_SOURCE=        # heirloom | heirloom_preferred; also requires bridge=on; unset = legacy reads
#
# ── Portable state directory ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Prefer AEGIS_RUNTIME_STATE_DIR over the checkout root for durable files.
# scripts/first-run.sh wires MEMORY_DB_PATH / IDENTITY_KEY_PATH /
# CREDENTIAL_DB_PATH (opt-in heirloom lives next to MEMORY_DB_PATH) under:
#   ${AEGIS_RUNTIME_STATE_DIR:-${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/aegis-runtime}
# Pid + log already live there via scripts/aegis-runtime-local.sh.
# For live database backups use aegis_runtime_backup_db; for stopped-state
# migration copy the directory and .env, retaining the DB key separately.
# To keep data in the checkout, set the three *_PATH vars explicitly to ./….
# AEGIS_RUNTIME_STATE_DIR=

# ── Test / CI only (never set these in a real deployment) ────────────
# AEGIS_LIVE_XAI_IMAGE_SMOKE, AEGIS_UPDATE_CONTRACT_FIXTURES,
# HERCULES_E2E_TASK_FILE — opt-ins for the author's live/contract tests
# and have no meaning outside them.
# Managed phone access (set by scripts/remote-access.py; local/private/public).
# AEGIS_ACCESS_MODE=local
# AEGIS_PUBLIC_RISK_ACCEPTED=  # public consent is set only after explicit selection
